1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,626
A delayed truck puts the next pickup at risk.

2
00:00:03,706 --> 00:00:11,449
RoadStar lets a dispatcher rehearse the recovery, approve a feasible replacement, and keep the driver and detention evidence connected.

3
00:00:11,529 --> 00:00:13,920
This is the working synthetic rehearsal.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,528
A carrier is coordinating loads, drivers, trucks, trailers, appointment windows, and duty time.

5
00:00:19,608 --> 00:00:23,842
A dock delay changes several of those commitments at once.

6
00:00:23,922 --> 00:00:26,861
RoadStar brings that decision into one workspace.

7
00:00:26,941 --> 00:00:33,332
The dispatcher can see the affected assignment and compare a replacement before changing the plan.

8
00:00:33,412 --> 00:00:35,920
Every demonstration shipment is explicitly synthetic.

9
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,349
Here, Alex is delayed on the first trip.

10
00:00:39,429 --> 00:00:43,634
Recording the expected departure exposes the next assignment at risk.

11
00:00:43,714 --> 00:00:47,920
The original commitment stays visible while we prepare a recovery.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,777
We select Morgan with a different truck and trailer.

13
00:00:51,857 --> 00:00:56,491
RoadStar checks availability, equipment, declared duty budgets, and truck-route travel time.

14
00:00:56,571 --> 00:01:02,491
The route comes from Valhalla using Ontario road data and the supplied vehicle dimensions.

15
00:01:02,571 --> 00:01:05,920
Missing evidence or an infeasible choice stays visible.

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,442
The recovery review shows the existing assignment, the proposed replacement, and the evidence used to make the comparison.

17
00:01:13,522 --> 00:01:15,532
The dispatcher approves this revision.

18
00:01:15,612 --> 00:01:20,547
At that moment, the server checks the current versions and availability again.

19
00:01:20,627 --> 00:01:25,144
If the driver or resource evidence changed, approval returns a conflict.

20
00:01:25,224 --> 00:01:30,995
A successful approval updates assignments, reservations, and the driver offer in one database transaction.

21
00:01:31,075 --> 00:01:33,920
Repeated commands retain their original idempotency key.

22
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,520
The driver receives a manifest with the next action, stop instructions, and trip context.

23
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,920
Acceptance is recorded separately from dispatch.

24
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:51,120
On the Android emulator, we also tested losing connectivity: acceptance stayed visibly pending, survived a force-stop, and synchronized after reconnecting without another assignment.

25
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:55,120
The native app preserves downloaded work and encrypted sign-in state.

26
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:57,520
Tracking is an explicit work-session choice.

27
00:01:57,600 --> 00:02:01,920
Physical phone background-location testing and native iOS verification are still deferred.

28
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,295
The same shipment keeps its document and detention context.

29
00:02:05,375 --> 00:02:09,420
This source PDF is stored privately, hashed, and extracted with Gemini.

30
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:12,795
A dispatcher reviews corrections without replacing the original extraction.

31
00:02:12,875 --> 00:02:15,045
Reviewed arrival instructions retain their source.

32
00:02:15,125 --> 00:02:20,295
Detention uses arrival and departure samples for the same stop and configured contract terms.

33
00:02:20,375 --> 00:02:22,920
The reviewer must acknowledge the observed-sample precision.

34
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,670
Approval creates a new invoice revision and preserves the draft.

35
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:31,920
It does not collect a payment or turn uncertain GPS into an exact timestamp.

36
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,804
RoadStar also plans shared routes.

37
00:02:33,884 --> 00:02:36,819
These two less-than-truckload shipments fit one selected vehicle.

38
00:02:36,899 --> 00:02:43,978
OR-Tools uses actual truck-route matrices to order both pickups and deliveries while checking the supported capacity and time constraints.

39
00:02:44,058 --> 00:02:46,616
The dispatcher reviews the assumptions before approving.

40
00:02:46,696 --> 00:02:51,891
The driver accepts one complete manifest, and the app enforces its global stop order.

41
00:02:51,971 --> 00:02:55,282
Vehicle reservations remain active until all deliveries are complete.

42
00:02:55,362 --> 00:02:57,920
This preview discloses its synthetic pallet-capacity allowance.

43
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,451
The importer reconciled all five supplied workbook sheets: over fifteen thousand source rows, including eighty-two exact duplicate dispatch rows.

44
00:03:04,531 --> 00:03:08,576
Original rows, missing fields, historical dates, and normalized records retain their lineage.

45
00:03:08,656 --> 00:03:10,295
Customer data remains isolated locally.

46
00:03:10,375 --> 00:03:13,732
The public preview uses synthetic scenarios for missing operational inputs.

47
00:03:13,812 --> 00:03:19,920
Scenario replay has its own clock and identical starting conditions, so modeled comparisons cannot overwrite live operational time.

48
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:27,349
The deployed platform uses Firebase identity, Cloud Run services, PostgreSQL with PostGIS, private Cloud Storage, Cloud Tasks, and Vertex AI.

49
00:03:27,429 --> 00:03:32,549
Database tests cover overlapping reservations, stale approvals, repeated commands, role boundaries, and uncertain telemetry.

50
00:03:32,629 --> 00:03:42,206
A cloud burst representing one hundred thirty-one drivers completed without failed commands, but measured about five point four seconds of synchronization lag at the ninety-fifth percentile.

51
00:03:42,286 --> 00:03:45,920
That is a measured limit, not a two-second service guarantee.

52
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,720
RoadStar connects recovery decisions to execution and evidence.

53
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:53,270
The next step is physical-device validation and carrier evaluation of the complete workflow.

54
00:03:53,350 --> 00:03:59,920
Open the preview to rehearse a recovery, inspect the assumptions, and follow the approved change through to the driver.
